WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.903
Stage sixteen: the honest pace.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:13.286
A climb and a flat run at the same effort don't share the same pace. Grade adjusted pace, GAP, makes them comparable.

3
00:00:13.386 --> 00:00:21.999
Kay's model, fitted on mountain race records, replaces Minetti's: nobody runs downhill twice as fast as on the flat.

4
00:00:22.849 --> 00:00:29.499
On the session page, the map switches to GAP mode: the track is coloured by grade adjusted pace.

5
00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:37.102
On the profile, raw pace sinks on the climb; the dotted GAP stays almost flat, the sign of an even effort.

6
00:00:37.952 --> 00:00:43.962
From Camille's best efforts, thirty seconds to two hours, comes her pace-duration curve.

7
00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:53.976
Between three and twenty minutes, a straight line gives her critical speed, four seventeen per kilometre, and her D′ reserve: ninety-five metres.

8
00:00:54.826 --> 00:01:01.689
Fewer than three durations, a single session, a curve too flat: the fit refuses, and says why.

9
00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:07.522
These are training efforts, not a test: critical speed comes out low, never flattering.

10
00:01:08.372 --> 00:01:16.451
For a threshold session, the target becomes four seventeen to four thirty, next to the heart-rate zone, never instead of it.

11
00:01:16.551 --> 00:01:25.143
Camille's watch puts her threshold at four oh one: a six percent gap. The coach flags it, with both values, without picking one.

12
00:01:25.993 --> 00:01:35.992
Twenty-nine degrees forecast on Saturday. The long run keeps its duration; the target pace slows by fifteen percent, heart rate stays put.

13
00:01:36.092 --> 00:01:42.358
And in the red, intensity is never kept: the session is moved, or lightened to endurance.

14
00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:48.514
Two documentation pages: grade adjusted pace, then critical speed.

15
00:01:51.908 --> 00:01:56.425
To go further: the “Critical speed” page of the documentation.
